caja is started in the same breath as the panel, before wayfire is ready to
serve clients: it stays alive but never creates the desktop window, so the user
gets a bare panel.  Started by hand a few seconds later in the same session the
desktop appears, which makes this a start-up ordering problem, not a caja bug
(caja does not use layer-shell; it creates plain wl_surface + xdg_toplevel).

The panel is a layer-shell client, so once it is running the compositor is
accepting clients.  Wait for it (bounded, so a broken session cannot hang) and
give wayfire one more second before handing caja to the restart loop.

--- a/session/mate-wayland-components.sh
+++ b/session/mate-wayland-components.sh
@@ -23,7 +23,20 @@
 restart_while_running polkit-mate-authentication-agent-1 &
 restart_while_running mate-notification-daemon &
 restart_while_running mate-settings-daemon &
-restart_while_running caja -n &
+# caja races the compositor: started in the same breath as the panel it stays
+# alive but never creates the desktop window, leaving a bare panel; started by
+# hand a few seconds later it works.  The panel is a layer-shell client, so its
+# presence means wayfire is accepting clients - wait for it (bounded) and give
+# the compositor one more second before caja comes up.
+(
+    i=0
+    while [ $i -lt 40 ]; do
+        pgrep -x mate-panel > /dev/null 2>&1 && break
+        i=$((i + 1)); sleep 0.25
+    done
+    sleep 1
+    restart_while_running caja -n
+) &
 
 #Programs to start once (if we have them), matching Xorg behavior
 nm-applet --indicator &
